"""Snapshot the free tier of several model gateways and diff each against last week.

Standard library only, because nothing here has needed more — not a rule
inherited from oxbox, whose reason for one does not apply to a public catalog.
Python 3.9+ (the system python3 on macOS is still 3.9).

    ./oxsurvey                        # snapshot every venue, print the diffs
    ./oxsurvey --venue zenmux         # one venue (repeatable)
    ./oxsurvey --dry-run              # fetch and diff, write nothing
    ./oxsurvey --diff A.json B.json   # diff two existing snapshots
    ./oxsurvey --list-venues          # what is configured, and how far to trust it

No API key is needed or used: every venue's catalog is public. Keys are for
calling models, which happens in oxbox, not here.

"Free" means the catalog reports zero for both prompt and completion. That
predicate catches cloaked listings too, which carry no `:free` suffix.

Two classes of venue, and the difference is load-bearing:

  Class A  the catalog carries pricing, so `free` is Measured. The snapshot
           holds the free models only.
  Class B  the catalog carries a roster but no pricing, so `free` is UNKNOWN
           and recorded as null — never as false, and never guessed from a
           `-free` suffix, because `big-pickle` and `grok-code` are free
           without one. The snapshot holds the whole roster, so that roster
           churn is still diffable.

Never let a class B null read as a measured zero. `roster_scope` and
`pricing_class` are written into every snapshot so a consumer can tell.

Alongside each snapshot, the raw catalog payload — every model as served,
pricing included, nothing filtered — is archived under catalogs/<venue>/.
Snapshots are the curated free-tier record; catalogs exist so a claim about a
paid or delisted model ("glm-5.3-flash cost $0.075/M on 2026-08-27") stays
checkable after the venue moves on.
"""

